note left of Customer
  <b>POLYMORPHISM</b>
  chooseMenuItem(), getSatisfactionLossPerTurn() and
  getTypeName() are {abstract} and dynamically bound;
  calculateBillCents() may be overridden.
  TurnMgr and Waiter call them on any Customer
  with no type checks.
end note

note top of Restaurant
  <b>COMPOSITION</b> (slide 36: the whole creates the parts)
  Restaurant's constructor creates its Tables,
  WaitingQueue, staff and Scoreboard;
  they cannot outlive it.
end note

note left of Order
  <b>ENCAPSULATION</b>
  status and unitsCompleted are private.
  Only advancePreparation(), markServed(),
  markPaid() and cancel() change them, and each
  checks the move is legal (no serve before READY).
end note

note top of Assignable
  <b>ISP / OCP</b>
  Only manager-directed staff realise this.
  Cashier is automatic, so it is not forced to
  implement perform(). GameController loops over
  List<Assignable>: a new directed role needs no
  change to the controller.
end note

note right of GameUI
  <b>DIP</b>
  GameController depends on this abstraction,
  not on Scanner / System.out. A scripted test
  UI (test/ScriptedUI) runs the same controller.
end note

note bottom of WaitingQueue
  <b>Delegation over inheritance</b>
  has-a List, not extends LinkedList:
  callers can only join / remove / look.
end note

legend bottom left
  <b>Notation (Chapter 6)</b>
  Association end: <i>role {ordered} multiplicity</i> at the navigable end, where the role is the
  attribute name in the code (slide 33); the other end shows its multiplicity only (slide 19).
  A line with a role at both ends is bi-directional: each class holds a reference to the other.
  Reference attributes are shown as association roles, not repeated in the class box.
  ◆ composition: the whole creates its parts · ◇ aggregation: parts added by reference (slide 36)
  Dashed arrow: dependency «create» / «call» / «use» (slide 34). All dependencies: stage1-dependencies-1..3 and stage1-dependencies.md.
